This prints Tomas, then 0. Trace the changed value through the same rule.

A freshly written object literal gets an excess-property check. This separate, intentionally failing program reports an extra visits property:
```typescript
type PersonName = { name: string };
function nameText(person: PersonName): string { return person.name; }
nameText({ name: "Asha", visits: 3 });
```
Do not paste this failing call into the working project. Naming an object first does not make a wrong name type safe: name: 3 still fails. Neither compatibility nor excess-property checking removes fields at runtime.

